{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1563425-hot-shotting","id":1563425,"title":"Hot-Shotting","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2026-08-24T12:54:52-07:00","id":1563425,"title":"Hot-Shotting","content":"We all know that Mage has a lot of weird rotes, often contradicting the core rules. I'd like to know if there is any rote that you feel is this way and what you do about it. Just ignore it? <br /> At the same time, I want to bring one present in M20:<br /> <br /> Hot-Shotting<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Anyone who\u2019s ever watched a movie from the last few decades recognizes the ominous \u201cCHK-CHK\u201d of a cocked shotgun, the click of a pistol\u2019s hammer, the \u201cSHHHING\u201d of a drawn blade, or the clack of a rifle-bolt slapping into place. By modifying her weaponry and then playing upon the psychological effect of media-instilled dread, a Technocratic operative can get her target to stand down, increase the efficiency of her attacks, or project a storm of fire so intense that only the strongest Reality Deviants can stand against it. System: A correctly trained operative \u2013 that is, one with the right Spheres \u2013 can use a simple Crafts roll to modify her weapon so that it makes more noise and gets a bit of extra kinetic kick. The Forces Sphere enhances both the sonic timbre of the sound and the kinetic damage of its strike. The resulting effects upon the target could be seen as psychological warfare, skillful applications of physics, or both. Each success reduces the difficulty for the agent\u2019s next attack by -1, and adds one automatic success for the Forces Sphere. If she so chooses, the player may apply that bonus to an intimidation-based attack (to get the target to surrender without a fight); to damage (thus hitting the target harder than usual); to targets (allowing her to hit more than one of them with the next attack); or to a mixture of them all. For details, see the Optional Dividing Successes Rule, p. 538. The addition of Mind 2 turns the Hot-Shotting Adjustment into an explicit psychological threat. In this case, the ominous sound is enough to make most targets surrender right away. The player rolls against a difficulty of the target\u2019s Willpower; if her successes equal or exceed the target\u2019s Willpower, then the target gives up without a fight. If not\u2026 well, then, that first shot still has a lot more force to back up the agent\u2019s argument. </div> </div> </div> So, while default rules says that you can increase/decrease the damage of an attack with Forces at the rate of 1:1; or increase/decrease difficulties also at the rate of 1:1, this rote seems to have its own rule.<br /> <br /> Each success grants you a -1 diff reduction (although the defaul limit of -3 should apply) AND a bonus in damage, intimidation or targets in your next attack.<br /> <br /> Thoughts?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-24T13:41:48-07:00","id":1563429,"title":"","content":"Euthanatos Tradition Book has a couple of real jewels. Almost all have something.<br /> <br /> Here's some from <i>Revised</i>.<br /> <br /> <b>Death in Life</b>, among a tree of effects that don't make sense with the amount of successes required and such, allows you to <b>halve incoming bashing damage </b><br /> <br /> This one it's interesting because it's ok to say that Spheres can change the nature of your body, and it will take the properties of whatever you morph into. You have to know that <i>undead halve bashing damage</i><br /> <br /> <b>Gift of Prana</b><br /> Each success creates a &quot;pool&quot; of two Health Levels, that then get spent when you suffer damage to heal you. It's understood as an enchantment (like a Charm?), and if it was ruled like a Wonder it would be ok, but it isn't using Wonder creation rules nor it's considered a Wonder.<br /> <br /> The mere idea that this sort of &quot;complex programmed effect&quot; it's possible w/o spending all you need to spend to create a Wonder...<br /> <br /> <i>On 2 edition</i><br /> <br /> <b>Burning Lotus</b><br /> Sacrifices the mage on a big explosion that disintegrates everything on it's aoe (about 22 feet per success - which it's ruled ... where?), then as a reward for dying it doesn't just cause the normal damage but adds one extra health level of damage per point of Quintessence on your Quintessence Wheel. <hr /><br /> In general I treat them as &quot;secrets of the trade&quot; of the Tradition (or group of mages). Stuff that only they know how to do. And if a player wants to create something like that, it would have to be a Rote carefuly studied to the effect (aka, give me time to think)<br /> <br /> But it annoys me.<br /> <br /> It like: Yeah, Mage has rules - but they don't do what we want so we will ignore them or make something up. And we won't say how you can do the same, just have some imagination.<br /> <br /> Some &quot;feel&quot; like *hidden rules* - like you just *have to know* that if you give your body properties of the undead then you halve bashing (and what about treating bullets as bashing? - that's not how undead work on Mage?). Don't try to extrapolate from this that you can do a different spell to halve Lethal or Aggravated.<br /> <br /> An interesting &quot;hidden rule&quot; it's how numerous Rotes that cause damage at level 5 (Antimater from the Etherites and the Dragon Fire from Cruzade are examples, if I'm not mistaken) cause more damage than what the table indicates. This is sort of a <i>necessity </i>because otherwise spells that require higher level Spheres would cause <i>less</i> damage (because of the increased base difficulty) - but of course nothing in the rules suggests this is the case (1e did had rules on this regard, and so does DA: Mage).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-27T08:26:48-07:00","id":1563535,"title":"","content":"I agree with you, that also annoys me.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1563425-hot-shotting?p=1563429#post1563429\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Gift of Prana</b><br /> Each success creates a &quot;pool&quot; of two Health Levels, that then get spent when you suffer damage to heal you. It's understood as an enchantment (like a Charm?), and if it was ruled like a Wonder it would be ok, but it isn't using Wonder creation rules nor it's considered a Wonder.<br /> <br /> The mere idea that this sort of &quot;complex programmed effect&quot; it's possible w/o spending all you need to spend to create a Wonder...</div> </div> </div> </div>Maybe it's just an always running effect that heals you whenever you get hurt. It just doesn't spend the pool when not healing. It would not be ok if you could choose when to heal, though. <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1563425-hot-shotting?p=1563429#post1563429\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In general I treat them as &quot;secrets of the trade&quot; of the Tradition (or group of mages). Stuff that only they know how to do. And if a player wants to create something like that, it would have to be a Rote carefuly studied to the effect (aka, give me time to think)</div> </div> </div> </div>What if it is not a Tradition rote, like the Hot-Shotting example?<br /> <br /> And what you mean by carefuly studied? The character would have to spend time creating it?<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-27T09:06:12-07:00","id":1563538,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Revan</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Maybe it's just an always running effect that heals you whenever you get hurt. It just doesn't spend the pool when not healing. It would not be ok if you could choose when to heal, though.</div> </div> </div> </div>I do guess the idea was to rule these sort of &quot;regeneration&quot; effects using some combo of Duration and the optional rule of Ablative Successes.<br /> <br /> But there's no real rule to create these &quot;pools&quot; that get expended conditionally.<br /> <br /> Closest it's how you can &quot;delay&quot; a damaging effect so it takes place over several time units (dividing the damage between the different turns), but to <b><i>condition </i></b>the release of damage it's more than that.<br /> <br /> If these &quot;pools&quot; can be created, I could, for instance, make a spell that saves a pool of dice to resurrect me when I die w/o Time or anything else. It just &quot;an always running effect that ressurrects me whenever I die&quot;<br /> <br /> Then what would prevent me to make an &quot;always running&quot; spell that transforms me into a &quot;combat form&quot;, &quot;when I say X word with X intent&quot; ? - thus completely replacing the Time Trigger<br /> <br /> I think it's an interesting idea to extrapolate stuff on - I just wish it was an actual rule instead of an oddity. And if I had to make a rule about it, I would probably &quot;upscale&quot; the Time Trigger so it offers some extra advantage appropiate to requiring an extra Sphere. <hr /><br /> It's not going to be a fast-cast, spur of the moment thing, and it needs to give me real-life time to consider it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 09-01-2026, 09:21 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-28T13:23:11-07:00","id":1563577,"title":"","content":"Hot Shotting is confusing in its verbiage but I think the problem is two words.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>M20 Hot Shotting</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Each success reduces the difficulty for the agent\u2019s next attack by -1, and adds one automatic success for the Forces Sphere</b>. If she so chooses, the player may apply <span style=\"color:#c0392b\"><b>that bonus</b></span> to an intimidation-based attack (to get the target to surrender without a fight); to damage (thus hitting the target harder than usual); to targets (allowing her to hit more than one of them with the next attack); or to a mixture of them all. For details, see the Optional Dividing Successes Rule, p. 538.</div> </div> </div> </div>The problem words are <b>&quot;</b>that bonus<b>&quot;</b> because it makes the bit about damage and targets sound like something it is not.. Really, damage and targets just come from the Optional Dividing Successes rule. If that rule is in play the mage is already able to spend successes on targets or damage. &quot;That bonus&quot; makes it sound like the reduced difficulty is somehow buying extra targets or damage.<br /> <br /> Tell me, if instead of saying &quot;that bonus&quot; the section said &quot;those successes,&quot; how would you interpret the rote?<br /> <br /> I'd actually say the harder part is the next bit with the optional Mind addition, because that makes it sound like you first make a mental attack, but if that fails (or even if it doesn't) you get to use the same successes to boost the shot.<br /> <br /> There's a similar problem in the next rote, Hands of Death. The basic rules are using Life or Forces or both in increase the damage of a punch and the mechanics are adding damage to a punch as per the Damage and Duration table. No problem there.<br /> <br /> However, the optional Entropy add on lets the mage exploit weak points to reduce armor, durability, or soak by one level per success. Here there is no mention of splitting successes, so does each success do damage AND remove one level of armor, durability, or soak?<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-29T09:39:22-07:00","id":1563599,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1563425-hot-shotting?p=1563577#post1563577\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hot Shotting is confusing in its verbiage but I think the problem is two words.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> The problem words are <b>&quot;</b>that bonus<b>&quot;</b> because it makes the bit about damage and targets sound like something it is not.. Really, damage and targets just come from the Optional Dividing Successes rule. If that rule is in play the mage is already able to spend successes on targets or damage. &quot;That bonus&quot; makes it sound like the reduced difficulty is somehow buying extra targets or damage.<br /> <br /> Tell me, if instead of saying &quot;that bonus&quot; the section said &quot;those successes,&quot; how would you interpret the rote?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I already was interpreting as &quot;those successes&quot;, I don't think it changes anything, though. <br /> <br /> The problem is that the dividing successes rule applies to direct damage effects, afaik. Which means Violence as a Focus. While this role is not the usage of the attack as a focus, but an effect to give bonus to a weapon. The attack is made the next turn, in a regular attack way. That's what made it confusing to me.<br /> <br /> Unless I'm wrong and one should also apply the Dividing Successes rule to buffing spells. Let's say I want to buff a sword so it swings faster and harder, would the damage bonus be applied at a 1:2 ratio following the spell Damage rule?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1563425-hot-shotting?p=1563577#post1563577\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd actually say the harder part is the next bit with the optional Mind addition, because that makes it sound like you first make a mental attack, but if that fails (or even if it doesn't) you get to use the same successes to boost the shot.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, this makes even more confusing. The way I read it is exactly what you said. It's 2 effects in one. You try to intimidate with Mind and if it fails you roll damage.<br /> <br /> But then, what if you succeed? The shot doesn't deal damage? Maybe the Mind effect is on the loading/swinging weapon sound and you can choose if you carry on with the attack or not?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1563425-hot-shotting?p=1563577#post1563577\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's a similar problem in the next rote, Hands of Death. The basic rules are using Life or Forces or both in increase the damage of a punch and the mechanics are adding damage to a punch as per the Damage and Duration table. No problem there.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I wish the book made it clearer that it's a Violence as Focus rote and the attack is indeed an effect. Everything points to that direction, though, and I'd say there's no much doubt about that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1563425-hot-shotting?p=1563577#post1563577\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">However, the optional Entropy add on lets the mage exploit weak points to reduce armor, durability, or soak by one level per success. Here there is no mention of splitting successes, so does each success do damage AND remove one level of armor, durability, or soak?</div> </div> </div> </div>But then, as you pointed out, the same thing happens, 2 effects in 1. The raw clearly says that you get both the damage effect AND the Entropy bonus.<br /> <br /> Maybe rotes are supposed to be more powerful and able to provide bonus better than the default rules? Nothing in the rotes section implies that, though\u2026<br /> <br /> Did this happen in previous editions rotes too or it's just a M20 thing?<br /> <br /> Just an observation, the Hot-Shotting rote from revised (guide to the technocracy) provides only the buff to the weapon damage in a 1:1 base, which makes sense. <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3153-lord-revan","name":"Lord Revan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3153_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-08-31T07:47:34-07:00","id":1563687,"title":"","content":"There's nothing in the description of rotes for previous editions or M20 that indicates they are inherent more powerful or have a different system for using successes. The benefits mostly center around avoiding the fast-cast penalty and naturally working well with the user's paradigm. <br /> <br /> While M20 and other editions will often state different options for spending successes on effects (reducing or raising the difficulty of another roll, subtracting or adding successes to another roll, damage, duration, range, etc.) I don't think any of them have ever comprehensively set out a complete list or general rules for how these interact.<br /> <br /> One further issue is that often extra spheres are added in for seemingly good reasons, but without being flexible with success use we can't represent those reasons mechanically. The 'Entropy to exploit weak points' idea we just saw in Hands of Death is a good example, because if we just use the standard Violence as a Focus rules, it doesn't do anything.<br /> <br /> We can see this on pg 413, where Secret Agent John Courage's Stone Shattering Fist-Strike adds in Entropy 1, but just uses the basic success rules for damage, which is the same result as if the spell didn't include Entropy.<br /> <br /> But in other places, we can see the effect doubling up, such as in the Hands of Death rote, where successes are counted twice.<br /> <br /> Finally, we can see what I think is the most reasonable method, which is counting the successes towards the application like normal, but adding one extra effect for the use the Sphere. This I see on on page 440 where &quot;Damage-inflicting effects from the Entropy, Matter, and Prime Spheres undermines the Durability trait- Entropy by exploiting weak points...&quot; Here, in addition to the damage, Durability (an object's soak) is also reduced, but just by a single -2, it doesn't scale with # of successes.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}